% ============================================================================
% end-punctuation-mark — three marks that end a sentence and what each
% actually does (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Every sentence ends with ONE of a small set of marks, and which mark you
% pick depends on the sentence's job -- a period closes a plain statement,
% a question mark closes a question, an exclamation point closes a sentence
% said with strong feeling. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? end_punctuation_mark(period, $D)
%       % ends_a_declarative_sentence
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `end_punctuation_mark(mark, description)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on a punctuation-related term that is not one
% of these three. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and
% recall the mark it names:
%
%     ? end_punctuation_mark($M, communicates_that_a_sentence_is_a_question)
%       % question_mark
%
% The three end-punctuation marks, as a little truth table:
%
%     mark                description                                       quote (verbatim)
%     ------------------  ------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     period               ends_a_declarative_sentence                       "It has one job: to end a declarative sentence."
%     question_mark        communicates_that_a_sentence_is_a_question        "They look like ? and they're used to communicate that a sentence is a question."
%     exclamation_point    makes_sentences_exciting                          "Much like the period, the exclamation point has one job: to make sentences exciting!"
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "Punctuation: The Best Guide
% to Using Punctuation Marks" article, which introduces each of these
% three end-punctuation marks in its own complete sentence. WebFetch-
% verified before writing (twice — the second pass specifically
% re-confirmed each sentence's exact wording via the surrounding
% paragraph). `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a reputable secondary
% reference, the same tier the sibling `noun-type.adj`/`verb-type.adj`/
% `pronoun-type.adj`/`preposition-type.adj`/`conjunction-type.adj`/
% `determiner-type.adj` (the other libraries in this directory) already
% use for their Grammarly citations. A punctuation mark that is NOT one
% of these three — "comma", a real mark the same page also covers, but
% one that belongs to a DIFFERENT category (a mid-sentence pause mark,
% not an end-of-sentence mark like the three tabled here) — is
% deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a description. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table end_punctuation_mark {
    columns mark, description

    row (period, ends_a_declarative_sentence)
    row (question_mark, communicates_that_a_sentence_is_a_question)
    row (exclamation_point, makes_sentences_exciting)

    source "It has one job: to end a declarative sentence."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/punctuation-capitalization/punctuation/"
    trust consensus
}
